Graph Switching

Graph Switching is a collection of small C programs for enumerating graphs
obtained by applying spectral switching once to a regular graph. The main
interface supports GM, WQH, and fixed irreducible catalogue methods. The
programs were written for experiments with strongly regular graphs.

  Development notice: Unlike the original code, graphswitching will be
  mostly vibe coded. Use it at your own risk.

Programs

  Program                    Switching operation                      Configuration
  -------------------------- ---------------------------------------- ----------------------------------------
  graphswitching             GM, WQH, and fixed catalogue methods     Runtime options; recommended interface
  graphswitching-explore     Repeated switching rounds                graph6 input; parallel runtime options

graphswitching supports at most 967 vertices. Its WQH method has a maximum
part size of 8. There are also four legacy programs gen_all_srgs,
gen_all_srgs_64vts, gen_all_srgs_wqh6, gen_all_srgs_wqh_generic which can be
ignored.

Quick start

Build all six programs:

    make

Run the default GM method. The number of vertices is inferred from the
matrix:

    ./graphswitching < adjacency-matrix.txt \
        > switched-matrices.txt

Use ./graphswitching --help to see every option.

See INSTALL for compiler requirements, installation options, and details
about configuring the specialized programs.

Input

The program reads an adjacency matrix from standard input. It consumes
exactly n * n occurrences of 0 or 1; all other characters, including
spaces and newlines, are ignored. For example, a matrix may be written with
one row per line.

The programs expect an adjacency matrix for a regular, simple, undirected
graph. They do not comprehensively validate those properties.

graphswitching infers n by counting the binary entries of a square input
matrix. It also accepts a leading n=<number of vertices> line. Use
--vertices N to specify the order explicitly.

Command-line interface

graphswitching uses GM switching by default. All configuration is optional:

    Usage: graphswitching [OPTION]...

      -m METHOD, --method=METHOD
      -n N, --vertices=N
      -p P, --part-size=P
      --sym[=auto]
      -f FORMAT, --format=FORMAT
      -i FILE, --input=FILE
      -o FILE, --output=FILE
      -h, --help
      -V, --version

--help documents every option and accepted switching method, the defaults,
input and output formats, limits, examples, and exit statuses. Both
--option=value and --option value forms are accepted for options with
values; short options accept both -x value and -xvalue.

For example, explicitly request WQH switching with parts of size 3:

    graphswitching --method wqh --part-size 3 \
        --input adjacency-matrix.txt --output switched-matrices.txt

Method names identify families and `--part-size P` supplies their parameter:

  Method     Supported P       Catalogue name
  ---------- ----------------- ----------------------------------
  gm         2, 3, 4           GM4, GM6, GM8
  wqh        1 through 8       WQH with parts P,P,n-2P
  ah         3, 5              AH6, AH10
  gm2        2                 GM4,4
  is3        4                 IS8(level 3)
  is5        3, 4              IS6, IS8(level 5)
  fano       not used          Fano

The default is `--method gm --part-size 2`. Except for generic WQH, the
larger methods enumerate stored irreducible switching subgraphs. The
matrices and catalogues were adapted with permission; docs/methodology.md
records their provenance and compares the search methodologies. For example:

    graphswitching --method gm --part-size 3 < graph.matrix  # GM6
    graphswitching --method gm2 --part-size 2 < graph.matrix # GM4,4
    graphswitching --method is5 --part-size 4 < graph.matrix # IS8(level 5)

For highly symmetric inputs, a build with nauty support can search
representatives of the switching-set or partition orbits:

    graphswitching --method gm --sym \
        --input adjacency-matrix.txt --output switched-matrices.txt
    graphswitching --method wqh --part-size 4 --sym \
        --input adjacency-matrix.txt --output switched-matrices.txt

`--sym` computes Aut(G) once with nauty and uses method-specific stabilizer
orbits to retain switching representatives. It preserves the set of output
isomorphism classes, but not raw output multiplicity or ordering, and it is
not a general isomorphism filter. See docs/symmetry-search.md for pseudocode.
`--sym=auto` also computes Aut(G), but uses orbit enumeration when the group
order is at least 5; otherwise it runs the ordinary search. Plain `--sym`
always forces orbit enumeration. Both modes require nauty support.

WQH switching with parts of size 2,2,n-4 corresponds to four-vertex GM
switching. The default uses the dedicated enumeration from gen_all_srgs,
which considers each four-vertex switching set directly. Use
--method wqh only when the WQH partition enumerator is specifically
desired.

The project version is recorded in VERSION and is available at run time:

    graphswitching --version

Output

Output is written to standard output. The default matrix format starts with:

    n=<number of vertices>

It is followed by the adjacency matrices found by one switching step, with
a blank line after each matrix. The search can produce isomorphic graphs;
the programs do not remove duplicates up to isomorphism. The --sym option
can remove duplicates caused by equivalent switching sets or partitions,
but it is not a general graph-isomorphism filter.

The matrix output is compatible with the adjacency-matrix tools from
nauty and Traces (https://pallini.di.uniroma1.it/). For example, when the
prefixed gtools names are installed:

    ./graphswitching < adjacency-matrix.txt \
        | nauty-amtog -q \
        | nauty-labelg -gqt \
        | LC_ALL=C sort -u > switched.g6

Use `--format graph6` to write the same graphs directly as one graph6 record
per line, avoiding the `amtog` conversion step:

    ./graphswitching --format graph6 < adjacency-matrix.txt \
        | nauty-labelg -gqt \
        | LC_ALL=C sort -u > switched.g6

nauty and Traces are optional. During make, the build checks whether the
nauty headers and library are available. If they are, graphswitching gains
`--sym` and `--sym=auto` support; otherwise the same program is built without
those features and reports a clear error if either is requested. See INSTALL
for configuration examples.

Iterated graph6 exploration

graphswitching-explore repeatedly applies graphswitching to a frontier of
graph6 graphs. It canonically labels every result with nauty, discards every
isomorphism class seen in the seeds or an earlier round, and uses the new
classes as the next frontier.

Supply one or more graph6 files and an empty output directory. This example
runs WQH switching with part size 3 on at most eight graphs at once:

    graphswitching-explore \
        --method wqh --part-size 3 \
        --jobs 8 --rounds 5 \
        --output-dir switching-closure \
        seeds.g6 more-seeds.g6

Use - as an input file, or supply no input file, to read graph6 records from
standard input. graph6 headers and blank lines are accepted. Isomorphic seed
graphs are reduced to one canonical representative before the first round.

The output directory must be new or empty. The program creates:

    initial.g6
        Canonical, pairwise nonisomorphic seeds.

    round-0001.g6, round-0002.g6, ...
        Classes first discovered in that round. No graph in one of these
        files is isomorphic to a graph in an earlier file.

    all.g6
        The accumulated union of initial.g6 and every completed round file.

The default is ten rounds and one parallel worker per available CPU. --jobs
controls both pipeline stages: up to that many frontier graphs are switched
concurrently, and the same number of labelg and sort lanes are apportioned
among the active graphs. When a round has fewer frontier graphs than workers,
each graph's output is divided between multiple labelling lanes. --processes
is an alias for --jobs. There is no default wall-clock or graph-count limit.
Add either or both limits; the first reached limit stops the run:

    graphswitching-explore \
        --rounds 20 --time-limit 3600 --max-graphs 100000 \
        --output-dir switching-closure seeds.g6

A run also stops early when a round finds no new class. The graph limit
counts the canonical seeds and all retained new classes. If a time limit is
reached during a round, completed worker results are saved and active
graphswitching processes are terminated.

Newly generated classes can be restricted by automorphism group size:

    graphswitching-explore \
        --aut-size 4 \
        --output-dir aut-4 seeds.g6

    graphswitching-explore \
        --min-aut-size 4 --max-aut-size 128 \
        --output-dir aut-4-to-128 seeds.g6

The supplied seeds are always retained and form the first frontier; the
filter applies to newly generated classes. This invariant is computed with
dreadnaut. The equality option cannot be combined with the lower or upper
bound options.

graphswitching-explore requires Python 3, labelg, and the standard sort
utility. It requests graph6 output directly from graphswitching, so amtog is
not required. Automorphism filtering additionally requires dreadnaut. The
program recognizes both unprefixed and nauty-prefixed gtools names. The
LABELG, DREADNAUT, GRAPHSWITCHING, and SORT environment variables, or the
corresponding command-line options, select nonstandard installations. Use
--nauty-prefix to choose a common command prefix.

Pass --sym to use graphswitching's automorphism-orbit search inside every
step, with either GM or WQH. This is independent of the automorphism group
size filter: --sym reduces switching sets or partitions, while the filter
decides which newly generated graph classes enter the next round.

Project layout

-   include/graphswitching.h contains the public C interface.
-   src/graphswitching.c contains the public API and search dispatcher.
-   src/gm.c, src/wqh.c, and src/fixed.c contain the three search engines.
-   src/symmetry.c contains automorphism and orbit handling.
-   src/io.c contains matrix input and matrix/graph6 output.
-   src/switching_methods.c contains the fixed switching matrices and
    irreducible switching-subgraph catalogues.
-   docs/methodology.md records provenance and compares search methodologies.
-   docs/symmetry-search.md documents the symmetry algorithms in pseudocode.
-   tools/graphswitching.c contains the command-line interface and its
    buffered output setup.
-   tools/graphswitching_explore.py contains the parallel graph6 exploration
    driver.
-   code/ contains the unchanged original standalone programs.
-   tests/ contains regular-graph inputs used to compare the new tool with
    the original, plus a generator/validator for the algebraic fixtures.
-   benchmarks/ contains reproducible end-to-end timing cases.

The command-line parser, graph I/O, symmetry support, and search families are
separate modules so each can evolve without restructuring the others.

Specialized builds

The specialized source files hard-code the graph order. Edit the relevant
#define VTS and rebuild that target:

    make clean
    make gen_all_srgs

gen_all_srgs_64vts stores each adjacency row in one INTT; consequently,
VTS must not exceed the number of bits in that type. See the comments in
code/gen_all_srgs_64vts.c before changing VTS or INTT.

Performance

The programs enumerate candidate switching sets combinatorially, so running
time and output size can grow quickly with the number of vertices and the
part size. graphswitching writes complete rows and uses a larger standard
output buffer to reduce output overhead. The default build also uses
optimization and loop-unrolling flags. Override them when needed, for
example:

    make clean
    make CFLAGS="-O2"

To compare several conservative GCC optimization profiles across a dedicated
graphswitching tuning category and rebuild it with the fastest one:

    make tune-gcc

See benchmarks/README for the tested profiles and tuner options.

On graphs with a large automorphism group, --sym can reduce both
the candidate search and output by orders of magnitude. Computing nauty
groups and set stabilizers has overhead, so the ordinary enumerator can
still be faster for asymmetric graphs or very small searches.

Benchmarks

The benchmarks/ directory contains reproducible benchmarks. They time the
switching program by itself, then separately canonically label its output,
remove duplicates, and verify the number of isomorphism classes. Direct
graph6 output is used for graphswitching; legacy matrix programs still pass
through `amtog`.

The default `short` suite is designed to finish within one minute on the
reference machine. `full` adds every reasonably quick ordinary, symmetry,
paper, and nonisomorphic-witness case. `extrafull` runs every case,
including the roughly eleven-minute legacy Sp(4,4) baseline:

    make benchmark
    make benchmark BENCHMARK_SUITE=full BENCHMARK_RUNS=3
    make benchmark BENCHMARK_SUITE=extrafull

Run named cases independently of the selected suite with:

    make benchmark BENCHMARK_CASES="sp6-wqh-p2 paley73-wqh-p3"

The runner automatically recognizes both the unprefixed nauty program names
(amtog, labelg) and the names used by some Linux distributions
(nauty-amtog, nauty-labelg). See benchmarks/README for explicit
overrides and a description of every case.

Background

Examples, data sets, and more context about the strongly regular graph
computations are available on
Ferdinand Ihringer's SRG page (https://ihringer.org/srgs.php).
The more fancy switchings are based on the work by Simoens and
Van Overberghe ( https://github.com/robinsimoens/implementing-switching-methods ).

